The 'Small File Problem' is a major anti-pattern in distributed storage systems like HDFS or AWS S3.
It occurs when you store data as millions of tiny files (a few kilobytes each) rather than grouping it into fewer,
optimally sized blocks (typically 128MB to 1GB).

Technical Breakdown :- 

Metadata Overhead: Distributed file systems separate data from metadata.In HDFS, the NameNode stores the metadata for every single file in its RAM.
If you have 10 million 1KB files, you will exhaust the NameNode's memory and crash the cluster long before you run out of actual disk space.
On cloud storage like S3, millions of files mean millions of API calls just to list the directory.

I/O Inefficiency: Analytics engines like Spark or Presto are built for sequential I/O—they want to open a large file and stream the data.
If they have to process 10,000 tiny files, the system spends more time opening connections,
reading the file header, and closing the connection than it does actually reading the data payload.

Buisness Context :- 
From a business standpoint, this problem causes cloud bills to skyrocket. Cloud providers charge per PUT and GET request.
Ingesting a streaming firehose as millions of tiny JSON files will run up a massive API bill,
while simultaneously causing end-user BI dashboards to time out because the query engine is choking on the metadata listing.

Design Decision :-
To fix this in a modern pipeline, I implement automated compaction. If we are streaming data in real-time, I'll let the tiny files land in a raw zone temporarily.
Then, I schedule a downstream batch job (or use a continuous maintenance process) to read those small files and rewrite them as large, compressed, columnar files like Parquet.
Even better, if I'm using a table format like Apache Iceberg or Delta Lake, I just run their native OPTIMIZE commands to handle the compaction automatically under the hood.

